1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,808
Everyone asks: what actually leaves my machine?

2
00:00:01,808 --> 00:00:06,000
Let's make the local-versus-cloud boundary concrete instead of hand-wavy — because with an agent, it matters.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,692
On your machine: the whole interface, all file and terminal work, every tool execution, Whisper transcription, local speech synthesis, the browser panel, and all your storage.

4
00:00:13,692 --> 00:00:18,000
Everything with hands — anything that touches your files or your system — happens locally, always.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,858
On the provider, if you use a cloud model: the model, and only the model.

6
00:00:20,858 --> 00:00:22,815
It receives the assembled prompt and returns text.

7
00:00:22,815 --> 00:00:29,000
It never touches your files directly — when the agent reads a file, that happens on your machine, and only what the model needs to see is put into the prompt.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,042
Here's the same question across five real setups.

9
00:00:31,042 --> 00:00:32,875
A local model with no tools: nothing leaves.

10
00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:34,917
Local model with local embeddings: still nothing.

11
00:00:34,917 --> 00:00:37,708
A cloud model: your prompts and the file snippets it's shown leave.

12
00:00:37,708 --> 00:00:39,833
Cloud embeddings, and cloud voice: those leave too.

13
00:00:39,833 --> 00:00:42,000
Match your setup to the row you're comfortable with.

14
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,739
And the row that surprises everyone, worth its own beat: cloud embeddings upload every document in a knowledge base to build the index — not just the passages that get retrieved.

15
00:00:49,739 --> 00:00:52,130
If a knowledge base is sensitive, use local embeddings.

16
00:00:52,130 --> 00:00:55,000
This is the single most misunderstood part of the privacy picture.

17
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:56,636
You now know how a turn works and where it runs.

18
00:00:56,636 --> 00:01:01,000
Next: the handful of prompting patterns that actually make a tool-using agent better — with before-and-after examples on camera.

